I"<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>AI techniques are increasingly used to predict cardiovascular disease in people with type 2 diabetes. What has been done in the last ten years, and what is missing?</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We searched the literature for English-language studies of AI models predicting cardiovascular disease in adults with type 2 diabetes. Five studies, with 560 to 203,517 participants, met our criteria.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>The models predicted risk over 5 or 10 years. Ensemble methods, especially random forests, were used most and performed consistently well. External-validation AUROC was 0.75 against 0.69 for conventional risk scores. But only one model had been externally validated, reporting quality was generally low, and none were reproducible or reusable.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>The promise is real, but without external validation and clear reporting these models cannot yet be trusted or reused in clinics.</p>
